WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:06.131
Vatican City, or the Vatican, as it is commonly known, lies within the city of Rome.

2
00:00:06.132 --> 00:00:10.383
Despite its name, Vatican City is actually a country!

3
00:00:10.384 --> 00:00:15.551
This country is home to the leadership of the Catholic Church and has a rich history.

4
00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:18.829
St. Peter's Basilica is its most famous site.

5
00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:24.350
The church was named for St. Peter, who was Jesus' favorite and most faithful follower.

6
00:00:24.351 --> 00:00:27.504
St. Peter was an early Christian leader in Rome.

7
00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:30.893
At that time, the Romans persecuted Christians.

8
00:00:30.894 --> 00:00:35.376
In the year 64, he was executed by Roman officials.

9
00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:39.240
He was buried in a graveyard on a hill called the Vatican.

10
00:00:39.241 --> 00:00:45.088
As the number of Christians grew, a large church was built on the site where St. Peter was buried.

11
00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:51.426
Soon St. Peter's Basilica became the main church of the pope, the leader of the Catholic Church.

12
00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:55.627
By 1506 St. Peter's Basilica was falling apart.

13
00:00:55.628 --> 00:00:59.602
It was also too small for all the church officials who worked there.

14
00:00:59.603 --> 00:01:05.969
So Pope Julius II demolished it and built a huge new church with a 42-meter-tall dome.

15
00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.458
This new church was not fully completed until 1626.

16
00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:15.538
The new St. Peter's Basilica became the largest church in the world at the time.

17
00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:21.907
And because of its connection to St. Peter, it also became one of Christianity's most important churches.

18
00:01:21.908 --> 00:01:24.403
The church is still in use today.

19
00:01:24.404 --> 00:01:28.812
Another famous religious building in the Vatican is the Sistine Chapel.

20
00:01:28.813 --> 00:01:33.865
The chapel was built between 1477 and 1481.

21
00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:41.122
The rectangular building looks plain and simple on the outside, but there is a treasure of Renaissance art inside.

22
00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:47.851
Pope Julius II needed an artist to decorate the chapel's ceiling—all eight hundred square meters of it.

23
00:01:47.852 --> 00:01:52.273
He hired Michelangelo for the job, which was a surprising choice.

24
00:01:52.274 --> 00:01:55.881
Michelangelo was known for his sculpture, not his painting.

25
00:01:55.882 --> 00:02:01.671
For four years Michelangelo painted the ceiling with his head tilted up and his hands extended above his body.

26
00:02:01.672 --> 00:02:04.822
His labor was an amazing feat.

27
00:02:04.823 --> 00:02:08.497
The result was an even more amazing work of art.

28
00:02:08.498 --> 00:02:12.387
The ceiling's nine main sections illustrate scenes from the Bible.

29
00:02:12.388 --> 00:02:17.544
Paintings of people and angels from the Bible decorate the borders.

30
00:02:17.545 --> 00:02:23.870
Thousands of tourists visit the Sistine Chapel each day to see Michelangelo's magnificent art.

31
00:02:23.871 --> 00:02:28.070
Like St. Peter's Basilica, the Sistine Chapel is still used today.

32
00:02:28.071 --> 00:02:34.908
In fact, the chapel is used for one of the Catholic Church's most important ceremonies—the election of a new pope.

33
00:02:34.909 --> 00:02:41.803
When a pope dies or resigns, church officials called cardinals come to the Vatican to choose their new leader.

34
00:02:41.804 --> 00:02:44.546
The cardinals stay locked inside the Vatican.

35
00:02:44.547 --> 00:02:48.974
They have no contact with the outside world until they elect a new pope.

36
00:02:48.975 --> 00:02:53.529
Every day during this time, the cardinals go into the Sistine Chapel.

37
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:57.051
They pray about which cardinal should be the next pope.

38
00:02:57.052 --> 00:03:00.979
Then elections are held each day until a new pope is chosen.

39
00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.417
The cardinals vote by writing a name on a piece of paper.

40
00:03:04.418 --> 00:03:08.685
The votes are counted, and then all the ballots are burned in the chapel's furnace.

41
00:03:08.686 --> 00:03:14.473
Outside the chapel, crowds gather, eagerly watching for signs of smoke from the chapel's chimney.

42
00:03:14.474 --> 00:03:18.579
Black smoke tells the crowd that the cardinals have not yet chosen a new pope.

43
00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:23.164
White smoke and the ringing of bells mean that a new pope has been elected.

44
00:03:23.165 --> 00:03:25.982
How is the black or white smoke made?

45
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:30.180
In the past, wet straw was added to the fire to turn the smoke black.

46
00:03:30.181 --> 00:03:34.590
But sometimes the smoke looked gray instead of black or white.

47
00:03:34.591 --> 00:03:39.122
So nowadays chemicals are used to produce the black or white smoke.

48
00:03:39.123 --> 00:03:44.659
After the election the new pope changes from his red cardinal's clothing into the white robes of the pope.

49
00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.277
Then he comes out and greets the people.

50
00:03:47.278 --> 00:03:52.432
He is now the leader of the Catholic Church—and a country, Vatican City.

51
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:55.079
Vatican City isn't just any country.

52
00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:00.637
It has a population of around only one thousand and covers less than half a square kilometer.

53
00:04:00.638 --> 00:04:03.698
This makes it the smallest country in the world.

54
00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:10.261
Vatican City is definitely small, but its history, art, and religious significance are huge.

55
00:04:10.262 --> 00:04:14.983
No wonder millions of people visit each year!

